steps.body:
When a function cannot do its job, it can `throw` an exception instead of returning a bad value. The standard exception types live in `<stdexcept>`: `std::runtime_error` for problems found while running and `std::invalid_argument` for bad inputs. Both are kinds of `std::exception`, so one `catch (const std::exception& e)` handles either.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

double safeDivide(double a, double b) {
    {{code}}
    return a / b;
}

int main() {
    try {
        std::cout << safeDivide(10, 4) << '\n';
        std::cout << safeDivide(1, 0) << '\n';
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if (b == 0) {
    throw std::invalid_argument("division by zero");
}
```

Wrap the calls that might fail in `try`, and handle the failure in `catch`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

double safeDivide(double a, double b) {
    if (b == 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("division by zero");
    }
    return a / b;
}

int main() {
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
try {
    const double q = safeDivide(1, 0);
    std::cout << q << '\n';  // skipped: the throw jumps out
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
    std::cout << e.what() << '\n';  // the message you threw
}
std::cout << "done\n";  // runs either way
```

A `throw` leaves the function at once, and every statement after it in the `try` block is skipped. After the matching `catch` finishes, the program carries on below it as if nothing happened.

> Gotcha: throw by value and catch by `const&`. A reference avoids a copy, and catching `std::exception&` still sees the real message. If nothing catches an exception, the program aborts with a nonzero exit code.

example.code:
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

double safeDivide(double a, double b) {
    if (b == 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("division by zero");
    }
    return a / b;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    try {
        const double first = safeDivide(10, 4);
        std::cout << first << '\n';
        const double second = safeDivide(1, 0);
        std::cout << second << '\n';
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << "done\n";
    return 0;
}

