        std::cout << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << "Balance: " << balance << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
The standard library throws too:

- `std::stoi(s)` throws `std::invalid_argument` when `s` does not start with a number, and `std::out_of_range` when the number does not fit in an `int`.
- `v.at(i)` throws `std::out_of_range` when `i` is not a valid index. `v[i]` never checks, so a bad index there is undefined behavior.

> Gotcha: `std::stoi` converts the leading number and stops at the first non-digit, so `std::stoi("2x")` is 2 and `std::stoi("12abc")` is 12. No exception at all.

One `try` can have several `catch` blocks. The first match wins, so put the most specific types first: a `catch (const std::exception&)` at the top would grab every standard exception before the specific blocks get a chance. `catch (...)` catches anything and goes last. An unused parameter can stay unnamed.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> colors = {"red", "green", "blue"};
    const std::vector<std::string> tokens = {"1", "abc", "7", "99999999999"};
    for (const std::string& token : tokens) {
        try {
            const int index = std::stoi(token);
            const std::string color = colors.at(index);
            std::cout << token << " -> " << color << '\n';
        {{code}}
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
} catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
    std::cout << token << ": not a number\n";
} catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
    std::cout << token << ": out of range\n";
} catch (...) {
    std::cout << token << ": unknown error\n";
}
```

Library `what()` text differs between compilers, so print your own message. Do the risky call before printing: in `std::cout << token << " -> " << v.at(i)`, the text `7 -> ` is already sent when `at` throws, and it stays in the output.

